#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168081 :tutorial/content "For two events $A$ and $B,$ **Bayes’ theorem** states that\n\n$$\nP(A|B)=\\dfrac{P(B|A)P(A)}{P(B)}.\n$$\n\nDeriving Bayes' theorem is straightforward. First, recall that the multiplication law states that\n\n$$\nP(A|B) = \\dfrac{P(A\\cap B)}{P(B)}.\\qquad\\qquad (\\ast)\n$$\n\nSwapping $A$ and $B$ in the multiplication law and noting that $P(B\\cap A) = P(A\\cap B),$ we have\n\n$$\nP(B|A) = \\dfrac{P(A\\cap B)}{P(A)}\n$$\n\nwhich can be written as\n\n$$\nP(A\\cap B) = P(B|A)P(A).\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above expression for $P(A\\cap B)$ into $(\\ast)$ gives Bayes' theorem.\n\nWe'll discuss the significance of Bayes' theorem shortly. But for now, let's get some practice applying the formula." :tutorial/id #uuid "4e1d5dc7-a377-56f0-b01f-288366126e34" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5146 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168135 :tutorial/content "It's possible to express Bayes' theorem in an alternative form using the law of total probability.\n\nFirst, let's restate Bayes' theorem:\n\n$$\nP(A|B)=\\dfrac{P(B|A)P(A)}{P(B)}\n$$\n\nNow, the law of total probability tells us that\n\n$$\nP(B) = P(B|A)P(A) + P(B|A')P(A').\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into Bayes' theorem, we get\n\n$$\nP(A|B)=\\dfrac{P(B|A)P(A)}{P(B|A)P(A) + P(B|A')P(A')}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1f7cc012-6b04-5ce7-a959-822f139b1557" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6467 :tutorial/title "Formulating Bayes' Theorem From the Law of Total Probability"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168136 :tutorial/content "Bayes' theorem states that\n![](images/e1/e1d65c5f59724230a371e63ff62bb8796d63bcb12e31ca7ff2b18bca00e3ba99.svg)\n\nFor reasons that will become clear shortly, we'll rewrite Bayes' theorem in a slightly different (but equivalent) form:\n![](images/ed/ed7bfbd78af1660b10856f6a78b66c8596b0b1dd0ade839bc910a6381475b98a.svg)\n\nBayes' theorem is significant because it lets us update our belief that a hypothesis is true when new evidence emerges. For this reason, it has numerous applications in science, computer science, and engineering.\n\nFor example, suppose a doctor wishes to assess the probability that a certain patient is infected with a particular disease. It is known that those aged ![](images/1f/1f1a501ced780d95ba1f0d886e696028662607bb0b37b13f9d7d3a1d1db60185.svg) or older are observed to have a higher probability of carrying the disease.\n\nLet's define the events ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) and ![](images/75/7533ad047439b7d799dd7afee5d6a7bc9f4404fbadb8c3f27ef6d3952119ff6b.svg) as follows:\n\n- ![](images/9d/9df014bbe82f5fd83bfb40f253da072a3873355ea7f25a2199a25f1385f0d171.svg) the event that a randomly selected patient has the disease\n\n- ![](images/3b/3ba12d975885bf1e318d35e51e6977174296f53bc4172ee24b458fcf0ea282a5.svg) the event that a randomly selected patient is ![](images/1f/1f1a501ced780d95ba1f0d886e696028662607bb0b37b13f9d7d3a1d1db60185.svg) years old or more\n\nThen, Bayes' theorem gives the following:\n![](images/26/26685b6a61d42e30e594cd99e0715abecd393aba35468887a6149dbf3305d9f0.svg)\n\nLet's introduce some new terminology:\n\n- ![](images/07/078062d17441bd681fcb9f3b8358e22f3c6460a2f25027a9379ee3e9f822cf97.svg) is called the **prior.** It tells us the probability that a randomly selected patient has the disease *without considering any other factors*.\n\n- ![](images/8d/8d07649dab5e4db06b27ee2c94bec75806ab4b8facf9f0994bcd482b0e4eff21.svg) is called the **posterior**. It tells us the probability that a randomly selected patient has the disease *taking into consideration* that they are ![](images/1f/1f1a501ced780d95ba1f0d886e696028662607bb0b37b13f9d7d3a1d1db60185.svg) or older.\n\n- ![](images/a7/a76e4b2e92fa0c3f3c0afed43f96387cbdd7db6f6e5fb915eb1f1d1807bfc927.svg) is called the **likelihood.** It tells us the proportion of infected people that are ![](images/1f/1f1a501ced780d95ba1f0d886e696028662607bb0b37b13f9d7d3a1d1db60185.svg) or older. This quantity could be estimated by taking a random sample from the population of infected people and computing the ratio of those who are ![](images/1f/1f1a501ced780d95ba1f0d886e696028662607bb0b37b13f9d7d3a1d1db60185.svg) or older.\n\nTherefore, Bayes' theorem lets us update the hypothesis (the patient has the disease) by incorporating information about the patient (they are ![](images/1f/1f1a501ced780d95ba1f0d886e696028662607bb0b37b13f9d7d3a1d1db60185.svg) or older) and available evidence (an estimate of the proportion of infected people who are ![](images/1f/1f1a501ced780d95ba1f0d886e696028662607bb0b37b13f9d7d3a1d1db60185.svg) or older). \n\nFurthermore, suppose the likelihood is updated at a later date (e.g., due to more evidence being available as more people become infected). In that case, this will further improve the accuracy of the posterior when future patients are assessed." :tutorial/id #uuid "dc2a4c3a-22a8-514b-af01-a3a5ce6e5725" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6468 :tutorial/title "The Significance of Bayes' Theorem"}]}
